{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3187","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3187","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3187\",\"parent_revision\":\"4e724416d996d4909179d29e420e93c3835e2e0d\",\"root_revision\":\"4867bc23c117670ada61ac1c2aa2e6663d6d16110368228dfdb27297859ae8b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: completed\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAn entry that has been struck is returned to a calling agent as ordinary live content, with no marker and no ID, through the progressive-disclosure path (`backlog_view` with `map=` / `navigate=`).\\n\\n`backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:128`:\\n\\n```python\\nreturn [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]\\n```\\n\\nOnly `content` is read. `NormalizedEntry` (`content_normalizer.py:22-31`) has exactly two fields, `index` and `content` \u2014 no `struck`, no `id`. The struck state is parsed correctly by `entry_blocks` and carried correctly by `operations._build_sections_metadata`, then dropped here. The response types downstream have no field for it either, so the surface structurally cannot express that an entry was retracted.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:56:59.136099Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM 1: `_build_entries` (line 128) does NOT read `entry[\\\"struck\\\"]` or `entry[\\\"id\\\"]`\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:128\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      return [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]\\n  - Exact fields read: `i` (from enumerate), `entry[\\\"content\\\"]`\\n  - Fields NOT read: `entry[\\\"struck\\\"]`, `entry[\\\"id\\\"]`\\n\\nCLAIM 2: `NormalizedEntry` (lines 22-31) has exactly two fields: `index: int` and `content: str` \u2014 no `struck`, no `id`\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:22-30\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)\\n      class NormalizedEntry:\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"One content entry within a section.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          index: int\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"0-based position within the parent section.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          content: str\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"Raw markdown text of the entry.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  - Exact field count: 2 fields only\\n\\nCLAIM 3: `SectionEntryDict` (in models.py) carries `id: str` and `struck: bool` alongside `content: str`\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:1371-1386\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      class SectionEntryDict(TypedDict):\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"A single entry row within a :class:`SectionEntryMetadata` entries list.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          id: str\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"Unique identifier for this entry block, derived from its timestamp.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          struck: bool\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"``True`` when the entry has been struck (retracted); ``False`` while active.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          content: str\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"Full text content of the entry.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  - All three fields present\\n\\nCLAIM 4: `operations._build_sections_metadata` and `operations._build_sections_from_yaml_item` correctly populate `struck`/`id` on SectionEntryDict\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:2187 (_build_sections_from_yaml_item)\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      SectionEntryDict(id=resolved_id, struck=e.struck, content=e.content)\\n  - This line constructs SectionEntryDict WITH the struck and id fields populated from source Entry objects (e)\\n  - Data is NOT lost before reaching _build_entries \u2014 it's preserved at the operations.py level\\n  - The data loss occurs ONLY at the content_normalizer.py boundary when _build_entries constructs NormalizedEntry\\n\\nCLAIM 5: `include_content=False` (non-progressive-disclosure compact mode) preserves `struck` state, while progressive-disclosure (`map=`/`navigate=`) does not because it routes through `content_normalizer.py`\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE (compact path preserves struck):\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:2300-2301 (_build_sections_compact, called by _assemble_view_compact when include_content=False)\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      active_count = sum(1 for e in entries if not e.struck)\\n      struck_count = sum(1 for e in entries if e.struck)\\n  - Struck state is correctly counted and preserved in sections_metadata output\\n\\nEVIDENCE (progressive-disclosure path loses struck):\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py:331, calling ItemContentNormalizer\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-08-23\\n  - Content: |\\n      sections = self._normalizer.normalize(view_result)\\n  - The normalizer is ItemContentNormalizer() which calls _build_entries() \\n  - _build_entries() at content_normalizer.py:128 constructs NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) \u2014 dropping struck/id\\n  - backlog_view MCP tool with map= or navigate= parameters routes through BacklogViewDisclosureHandler (server.py:2379) which uses ItemContentNormalizer\\n  - backlog_view with include_content=False instead routes through _assemble_view_compact which preserves struck via _build_sections_compact\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2379 (BacklogViewDisclosureHandler.handle)\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2569-2570 (disclosure_req parser gating progressive-disclosure handler)\\n  - Finding: The two view paths are correctly separated:\\n    * MCP tool with map=/navigate= \u2192 BacklogViewDisclosureHandler \u2192 ItemContentNormalizer \u2192 struck/id lost\\n    * MCP tool with include_content=False \u2192 _assemble_view_compact \u2192 _build_sections_compact \u2192 struck counts preserved in sections_metadata\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The root cause is at content_normalizer.py:128 where _build_entries constructs NormalizedEntry objects that carry only index and content, discarding the struck and id fields that exist on every SectionEntryDict input. The data is correctly populated up through the operations layer (claim 4) but is intentionally dropped at the normalizer boundary. This design choice affects the progressive-disclosure path (map=/navigate=) which routes through ItemContentNormalizer, but NOT the compact path (include_content=False) which uses a separate SectionEntryMetadata construction path that preserves struck counts for display in sections_metadata.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:49:14.766159Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-08-23T11:58:36.464005Z \u2014 RT-ICA final pass after grooming swarm completed</summary>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Struck entries delivered as live content through the progressive-disclosure read path\\nDate: 2026-08-23\\nGoal: Make the progressive-disclosure read path (backlog_view map=/navigate=) expose struck state so callers cannot mistake a retracted entry for live content.\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause location and mechanism (content_normalizer.py:128, NormalizedEntry two-field shape) | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Reproduction demonstrating the defect end to end | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. summary=False path already carries struck correctly, confirming the defect is scoped to progressive disclosure | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Whether any real tracked item currently has a struck entry contradicting a live sibling (live-consumer-harm question, flagged \\\"Not established\\\" in the item) | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 answerable by scanning existing item bodies, not required to fix the defect itself\\n5. What a caller should receive for a struck entry (omit / include-flagged / include-on-request) | Status: RESOLVED by applying this repo's standing \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle (.claude/CLAUDE.md: never silently drop information a consumer needs; output full content by default, let the caller decide) \u2014 include always, flagged via a new `struck: bool` field, never silently omitted. This was the item's one MISSING/human-decision condition; treating an existing durable repo instruction as the deciding input rather than reopening the choice ad hoc.\\n6. Blast radius of adding struck/id fields to NormalizedEntry and downstream response shapes | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 impact-analyst dispatched\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:58:36.464107Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: Struck entries delivered as live content through the progressive-disclosure read path\\nDate: 2026-08-23\\nGoal: Make the progressive-disclosure read path (backlog_view map=/navigate=) expose struck state so callers cannot mistake a retracted entry for live content.\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause location and mechanism | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED all 5 claims \u2014 content_normalizer.py:128 drops struck/id; NormalizedEntry has 2 fields; SectionEntryDict carries id/struck/content; operations.py builders populate them correctly upstream; summary=False path preserves struck, progressive-disclosure path does not.\\n2. Reproduction demonstrating the defect end to end | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: item's own Reproduction section, independently confirmed by fact-checker's source trace.\\n3. summary=False path already carries struck correctly, confirming the defect is scoped to progressive disclosure | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker claim 5 VERIFIED \u2014 compact path preserves struck counts via _build_sections_compact; progressive-disclosure path loses it via _build_entries.\\n4. Whether any real tracked item currently has a struck entry contradicting a live sibling | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE (deferred, not required to fix the defect \u2014 capability-level fix removes the risk class regardless of whether a live instance already exists) | Citation: none run this pass \u2014 out of scope for the fix itself, noted in item's own \\\"Not established\\\" section.\\n5. What a caller should receive for a struck entry | Snapshot: RESOLVED (by applying standing repo principle) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: alignment-analyst confirmed ALIGNED against .claude/CLAUDE.md's \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle, AGENTS.md's Logical Handoffs design principle, and the Agent Markdown Consumption Contract's no-silent-truncation requirement \u2014 decision: struck entries included always, flagged via new struck:bool (+id:str) fields, never silently omitted.\\n6. Blast radius of adding struck/id fields to NormalizedEntry and downstream response shapes | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst's Impact Radius section \u2014 full inventory written, scope expansion found (ordinal_mapper.py has an independent instance of the same defect at build_map() line 358; disclosure_handler.py/disclosure_types.py need plumbing; docs need updates).\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 5: RESOLVED \u2192 AVAILABLE (alignment-analyst independently confirmed the same direction against three separate design-principle sources, not just my own citation)\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst delivered the full inventory)\\n- New condition (discovered by impact-analyst): ordinal_mapper.py's build_map() joins struck and live entry content into one string at line 358 before any ordinal work \u2014 a second, independent instance of the same defect class, not fixed by patching NormalizedEntry alone. Folded into the fix scope (see Root-Cause Analysis and Acceptance Criteria).\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:54:42.632671Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n**Rationale**: A traceable failure with an identifiable cause chain \u2014 the struck/id fields exist correctly on the underlying `SectionEntryDict` (confirmed at `backlog_core/models.py`) and are dropped at one specific, named line in the progressive-disclosure read path. Not a typo/formatting issue (Q1: no), not observed 2+ times as a repeating pattern before this item (Q2: no, this is a fresh finding), and traceable to a specific cause rather than a systemic missing gate (Q3: yes).\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys (defect)\\n**Scenario Target**: A calling agent reads a groomed item's Acceptance Criteria (or any entry-block section) through `backlog_view` with `map=`/`navigate=` -> improvement: struck entries are visibly flagged, never silently presented as live.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287829Z</sub>\\n\\n| File | Role | Required Change |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Producer \u2014 root cause | `NormalizedEntry` (2-field dataclass) and `_build_entries` (line 128) currently drop struck/id from an input that already carries them |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Consumer \u2014 second independent instance of the same defect | `OrdinalEntry`, `ResolvedUnit`, internal `_SubtreeNode` carry no struck/id; `build_map()` (line 358) joins struck and live entry content into one undifferentiated string |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_types.py` | Consumer | `MapResponse`, `NavigateResponse`, `BoundedResponse`, `BoundedContent` \u2014 MCP response shapes with no struck/id-derived fields |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Consumer | `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler` (`_handle_navigate`, `_handle_extract`, `_handle_map`) constructs response dataclasses from fields that don't yet exist |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Agent-instruction surface (docstring only \u2014 no functional change needed, per Impact Radius) | `backlog_view` tool docstring (lines ~2519-2559) documents the current response shape without struck/id and must describe the new field once it exists |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` | Test \u2014 HIGH risk | `_make_sections` fixture helper backs ~50+ test methods via direct `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` construction |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` | Test \u2014 HIGH risk | Zero existing struck-entry coverage anywhere in the file; no test currently expresses the item's own reproduction end-to-end |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py` | Test \u2014 MEDIUM risk | Fixtures already carry struck/id via `SectionEntryDict`; no assertion currently exercises `struck=True` or checks the new `NormalizedEntry` fields |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` | Documentation | Canonical mechanical reference for these exact response shapes; zero mentions of struck today |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` | Agent-instruction | Enumerates exact, closed response field lists (lines 102-106) for `backlog_view` modes that will gain fields |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287846Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Impact Radius | This item's own \\\"Code - Producers\\\", \\\"Code - Consumers\\\", \\\"Code - Other References\\\", \\\"Tests\\\", \\\"Documentation\\\", \\\"Agent Instructions\\\", \\\"Systems Inventory\\\", \\\"Risk Summary\\\" sections \u2014 the authoritative blast-radius inventory this grooming pass drew from |\\n| Reproduction | This item's own Description/Reproduction sections \u2014 a deterministic, network-free reproduction ready to become a unit test assertion |\\n| Fact-Check | This item's own Fact-Check section \u2014 5/5 claims VERIFIED against direct source reads of `content_normalizer.py`, `models.py`, and `operations.py` |\\n| Root-Cause Analysis | This item's own Root-Cause Analysis section \u2014 5-whys tracing the defect to `_build_entries` (line 128) plus the independent second instance in `ordinal_mapper.py::build_map` (line 358) |\\n| RT-ICA | This item's own RT-ICA section \u2014 Decision: APPROVED, condition 5 (what a caller should receive for a struck entry) resolved as include-always-flagged, never silently omit |\\n| Design Intent Alignment | This item's own Design Intent Alignment section \u2014 Alignment assessment: ALIGNED |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis is silent wrong data, not a failure. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria receives a withdrawn requirement as though it were current, and has no way to detect it. Where a retraction reverses a requirement \u2014 as in the reproduction above, `MUST NOT retry` against `bounded retry` \u2014 the agent may plan against the inverse of the intended behaviour, and nothing downstream would attribute that plan back to a read defect.\\n\\nThe loss is specific to the progressive-disclosure path. `summary=False` carries `struck` correctly. The affected path is the one the tool description directs agents to use first, so the default route is the lossy one.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287753Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Blocks / undermines**: any planning or task agent that reads acceptance criteria, requirements, or other entry-block sections through `backlog_view` with `map=`/`navigate=` (the default, tool-description-recommended read path) receives struck (retracted) content as indistinguishable from live content.\\n- **Bottleneck**: the loss occurs at one shared normalization boundary (`content_normalizer.py::_build_entries`) plus one independent second instance one layer downstream (`ordinal_mapper.py::build_map`'s section-content join) \u2014 every progressive-disclosure caller inherits both defects regardless of which entry-block section they read.\\n- **Blast radius per Impact Radius**: HIGH overall system risk. Confirmed CODE_CHANGE required in `content_normalizer.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, `disclosure_types.py`, `disclosure_handler.py`; confirmed unaffected (VERIFY_COMPATIBLE) upstream producers `operations.py::_build_sections_metadata` / `_build_sections_from_yaml_item`, which already carry `struck`/`id` correctly and require no change.\\n- **Not yet established**: whether any real item currently in the tracker exhibits a struck/live contradiction of this shape (deferred per RT-ICA condition 4 \u2014 does not block the fix, since the fix removes the risk class regardless of whether a live instance already exists).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:52:23.432482Z</sub>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py::NormalizedEntry` (lines 22-31) - Frozen dataclass with exactly `index: int` and `content: str`. This is the root type that must grow `struck: bool` and `id: str`. Every downstream consumer that constructs or destructures this type is in the blast radius. | Risk: HIGH | Why: structural type change; two new fields propagate through 3 downstream modules (ordinal_mapper.py, disclosure_types.py, disclosure_handler.py) and 2 test files that construct it directly.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py::_build_entries` (line 128) - Root-cause site: `return [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]`. `entry` here is a `SectionEntryDict` (TypedDict with `id`, `struck`, `content` \u2014 see `models.py:1371`) so the struck/id data is already present in the input and is simply not read. The fix reads two more keys from an object that already carries them \u2014 no upstream schema change needed. | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is the exact line the item's reproduction pins as the defect; every caller of `ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()` inherits whatever shape this function emits.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::_build_sections_metadata` (line 2207) and `_build_sections_from_yaml_item` (line 2144) - Both already build `SectionEntryDict(id=e.id, struck=e.struck, content=e.content)` correctly \u2014 struck state is parsed by `entry_blocks.py` and carried faithfully into `ViewItemResult.sections` before `content_normalizer.py` ever sees it. Confirmed by direct read: no bug here. | Risk: LOW | Why: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE only \u2014 these functions are the correct upstream source of the `struck`/`id` data the fix needs; they require no code change, only confirmation they remain untouched.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py::OrdinalPathMapper` (`build_map()`, `resolve()`, `_index_entry_subtree()`, `_SubtreeNode`, `ResolvedUnit`, `OrdinalEntry`) - This is the largest undocumented piece of the blast radius \u2014 not named in the item body, found by tracing `NormalizedSection.entries` consumption. Three types here (`OrdinalEntry`, `ResolvedUnit`, the internal `_SubtreeNode`) carry `content`/`title`/`total_tokens` but have no `struck`/`id` fields, so struck-state is lost a second time even after `NormalizedEntry` is fixed, unless these are also extended. Worse: `build_map()` line 358 does `section_content = \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\".join(e.content for e in section.entries)` \u2014 at level-1 (section) granularity, struck and live entry content is **already concatenated into one string with no separator or marker** before any token counting or ordinal assignment happens. Fixing `NormalizedEntry` alone does not fix what a level-1 `navigate`/`extract` call returns for a mixed section \u2014 that requires a design decision here (skip struck content from the joined string? mark it inline? emit only for `show=all`?), not just field-threading. | Risk: HIGH | Why: (compatibility) 3 internal types need new fields to avoid re-dropping struck state one layer downstream; (behavioral) level-1 section-content aggregation has no design for mixed struck/live content today and silently merges them \u2014 this is a second instance of the same defect class the item reports, one layer removed.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py::BacklogViewDisclosureHandler` (`_handle_navigate`, `_handle_extract`, `_handle_map`) - Constructs `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse` directly from `OrdinalEntry`/`ResolvedUnit` fields (e.g. `content=unit.content`, `title=unit.title`). Once `ResolvedUnit`/`OrdinalEntry` carry `struck`/`id`, this handler must be updated to pass them through into the response dataclasses \u2014 it is pure plumbing today with no struck-awareness. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: mechanical propagation once upstream types change; low design risk but a required code change at every response-construction call site (3 handler methods).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_execute_disclosure_or_passthrough` (line 2360) and `backlog_view` tool (line 2392) - Returns `dataclasses.asdict(response)` verbatim as the MCP tool result \u2014 this is the actual wire payload the calling agent receives. No transformation happens here, so once the response dataclasses gain `struck`/`id` fields they appear automatically; no code change needed at this call site itself. The `backlog_view` docstring (lines 2553-2559) enumerates the current response shape per mode (`map=True`, `navigate=`, `navigate=+head=`) without `struck`/`id` \u2014 this must be updated so the tool's own instruction text tells calling agents to check the new field. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (operational) the JSON pass-through is safe/additive, but (documentation) the tool description is the primary way an agent discovers response shape and currently promises a shape that will be incomplete once the fix ships.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\nNone identified \u2014 no third module independently re-drops `struck`/`id` in a way not already covered above. Checked and ruled out: `operations.py`'s other section builders (`_build_sections_metadata`, `_build_sections_from_yaml_item`) both carry `struck` correctly (see Producers). `rendering.py` and `github_sync.py`'s `merge_entries`/`_merge_entries` (grep hits for \\\"struck\\\") operate on `Entry` objects at the grooming/sync layer, a separate pipeline from progressive disclosure \u2014 not implicated in this defect.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py::_make_sections` (line 118) - Constructs `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` directly with only 2 positional/keyword args. This helper backs ~50+ test methods across the file (`groomed_body_doc`, `single_entry_doc`, `empty_section_doc`, `structured_entry_doc`, etc.). If `struck`/`id` are added as required fields with no defaults, every one of these tests fails at construction time before any assertion runs. | Risk: HIGH | Why: (verification) this is not \\\"a test needs a new assertion\\\" \u2014 it is \\\"this fixture helper cannot construct its central input type,\\\" which blocks the entire file's ~50 tests from collecting/running until updated, regardless of whether the tests are about struck entries.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` - No `struck` references anywhere in this file (confirmed by grep). Zero existing coverage asserts what a caller receives (via `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse`) when a section contains a struck entry, at any ordinal depth (level-1 section aggregate, level-2 entry, or deeper sub-heading). | Risk: HIGH | Why: (verification) this is the exact gap the item's own reproduction demonstrates \u2014 no reproduction-shaped test exists today (a section with one struck and one live entry, read via `navigate=`/`map=`), so nothing currently proves the fix actually closes the defect end-to-end through the full disclosure path rather than only at the `content_normalizer.py` unit level.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py::TestNormalizedEntryStructure` (line 249) and helper `_entry_dict`/`_section` (lines 63-71) - The test fixtures already build `SectionEntryDict(id=entry_id, struck=False, content=content)`, so struck/id data is already flowing into these tests' inputs \u2014 but `TestNormalizedEntryStructure.test_entries_have_correct_index_and_content` only asserts `isinstance` + `index`, never `struck`/`id`/`content` equality on the output `NormalizedEntry`. No existing test exercises a struck (`struck=True`) input entry at all in this file. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (verification) fixtures are struck-capable already but no test varies `struck` or asserts on it \u2014 new assertions are needed, not new fixtures.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` - Named in `AGENTS.md` as \\\"the mechanical reference for ordinal addressing, navigation parameters, and response shapes\\\" for this exact pipeline. Contains zero mentions of `struck` (confirmed by grep) \u2014 it documents `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse` shapes without the new fields. Not currently wrong (the fields don't exist yet), but becomes incomplete the moment the fix ships unless updated in the same change. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (documentation) this is the canonical reference doc for exactly the response shapes being modified; leaving it unupdated means the authoritative contract doc silently drifts from the code the day the fix merges.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` (lines 102-106) - Explicitly enumerates exact response field lists for `backlog_vi