concern a different mechanism (grooming-layer `strike_entry`/`merge_entries` audit trail, and post-implementation markdown-strikethrough concern-tracking, respectively) \u2014 neither describes the progressive-disclosure read path and neither is contradicted by this change.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Role: producer | Connection: defines `NormalizedEntry`/`NormalizedSection` and the root-cause `_build_entries` function that drops `struck`/`id` | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'NormalizedEntry'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: consumes `NormalizedSection.entries`; builds `OrdinalEntry`/`ResolvedUnit`/`_SubtreeNode` with no struck/id fields; concatenates entry content at section level with no struck/live separation | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_types.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: `MapResponse`, `NavigateResponse`, `BoundedResponse`, `BoundedContent` dataclasses are the MCP response shapes that must gain struck/id-derived fields | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler` constructs disclosure response dataclasses from `ResolvedUnit`/`OrdinalEntry` fields at 3 call sites | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Role: producer (upstream, verified correct) | Connection: `_build_sections_metadata` and `_build_sections_from_yaml_item` already carry `struck`/`id` correctly into `SectionEntryDict` | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Role: consumer + agent-instruction | Connection: `backlog_view` tool passes disclosure responses through via `dataclasses.asdict`; tool docstring documents response shape without struck/id | Action: MULTIPLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` | Role: test | Connection: `_make_sections` helper constructs `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` directly; backs ~50+ test methods that will fail to construct fixtures if new fields are required without defaults | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'NormalizedEntry\\\\('\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` | Role: test | Connection: zero existing struck-entry coverage anywhere in the file; no test proves the item's reproduction scenario is fixed end-to-end through MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py` | Role: test | Connection: fixtures already carry struck/id via `SectionEntryDict` but no assertion exercises struck=True or checks the new `NormalizedEntry` fields | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: canonical mechanical reference for the exact response shapes being modified; currently silent on struck/id | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: enumerates exact, closed response field lists for `backlog_view` modes that will gain fields | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` - carries the same defect class one layer downstream of the reported bug (section-level content concatenation has no struck/live separation), and 3 internal types need new fields; not mentioned in the original item body \u2014 found only by tracing the actual consumer chain.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` - a single fixture helper (`_make_sections`) backs the majority of this file's tests via direct `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` construction; adding required fields without defaults blocks the whole file, not just struck-related tests.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` - zero existing coverage of struck-entry behavior anywhere in the MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT response path; the item's own reproduction is not currently expressible as a passing/failing test in this file.\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility (structural dataclass change propagates through 2 downstream modules before reaching MCP callers)\\n  - behavioral (section-level content aggregation in `ordinal_mapper.py` has no design today for mixed struck/live content \u2014 a second instance of the reported defect class)\\n  - verification (two test files have structural or coverage gaps that block or fail to prove the fix)\\n  - documentation (one canonical contract doc and one agent-instruction SKILL.md both enumerate response shapes that will go stale)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The item body names `content_normalizer.py` and \\\"the response types downstream\\\" generically. Tracing the actual consumer chain surfaces `ordinal_mapper.py` (`OrdinalEntry`, `ResolvedUnit`, `_SubtreeNode`) and `disclosure_handler.py` as concrete, previously-unnamed members of the blast radius, plus a second latent instance of the same defect class in `ordinal_mapper.py`'s section-level content-joining. Planning and fact-check scope should be expanded to explicitly include `ordinal_mapper.py`'s section-content-aggregation behavior, not just the `NormalizedEntry` field addition.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287862Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None. All required inputs are AVAILABLE per RT-ICA (Decision: APPROVED, MISSING count 0).\\n- Blocks: None known.\\n- Independent of #3145, #3147, #3174, #3168 \u2014 those items concern a different subsystem; this item's blast radius (per Impact Radius) is fully contained to the progressive-disclosure read path and its direct producers/consumers.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287730Z</sub>\\n\\nP0 confirmed.\\n\\nThe defect is silent wrong data, not a crash or an error a caller could notice and route around. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria through the progressive-disclosure path (`backlog_view` with `map=`/`navigate=` \u2014 the path the tool's own description directs agents to use first) receives a withdrawn requirement presented as though it were current, with no field in the response to distinguish it from live content. Where the retraction reverses the requirement (the reproduction's `MUST NOT retry` vs. `bounded retry`), the agent can plan against the inverse of the intended behavior, and nothing downstream attributes the resulting bad plan back to a read defect \u2014 there is no error to trace. This matches the P0 bar: undetectable data corruption on the default read path of a system other agents depend on for correctness.\\n\\nEffort: **M** (Medium). Impact Radius identifies 4 non-test files requiring a CODE_CHANGE (`content_normalizer.py` \u2014 root cause; `ordinal_mapper.py` \u2014 a second, independent instance of the same defect class at `build_map()`; `disclosure_types.py`; `disclosure_handler.py`), 1 file requiring an agent-instruction/docstring update only (`server.py`), 3 test files needing structural or coverage updates (one of which, `test_ordinal_mapper.py`, backs ~50 existing test methods through a single fixture helper and must not regress them), and 2 documentation files (`docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md`, `skills/backlog/SKILL.md`) whose closed field-list enumerations go stale once the response shapes change. Root cause, fix location, and scope are all fully understood (RT-ICA: APPROVED, MISSING count 0) \u2014 this is breadth-of-propagation effort (one field addition threaded through several already-identified call sites and two doc surfaces), not open-ended investigation effort.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287775Z</sub>\\n\\n- Acceptance-criteria (and any other entry-block section) reads through the default progressive-disclosure path become trustworthy: a planning or task agent can distinguish a retracted requirement from a current one instead of silently receiving both as equal peers.\\n- Removes the specific risk of plans being built against withdrawn or reversed requirements without any error signal to catch it \u2014 closes the exact failure mode demonstrated in the reproduction (a `MUST NOT` retraction indistinguishable from its contradicting `MUST` live sibling).\\n- Closes the same risk class in `ordinal_mapper.py::build_map()` found by impact-analyst \u2014 a second, independent instance of the identical omission one layer downstream, not mentioned in the original item body, where section-level content is joined into one string with no struck/live separation before any ordinal or token work happens. Fixing only `content_normalizer.py` would leave this second instance live; fixing both closes the full risk class rather than one instance of it.\\n- Brings the progressive-disclosure path to parity with the `summary=False` path, which already carries `struck` correctly \u2014 eliminates a two-path inconsistency where the same underlying data returns different fidelity depending on which read mode a caller happens to use.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:56:18.965325Z</sub>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **ALIGNED**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Rationale:**\\n\\nThe proposed fix direction (threading `struck`/`id` fields through `NormalizedEntry` and all downstream progressive-disclosure response shapes) aligns directly with three foundational principles:\\n\\n1. **\\\"No Invented Limits\\\" (project CLAUDE.md, lines 87\u2013103)**: The principle explicitly prohibits silent data loss (\\\"output full content by default \u2014 let the caller decide how much to read\\\"). Struck entries are currently silently dropped by `NormalizedEntry` in `content_normalizer.py:128`, violating this principle. The fix restores visibility of information agents need.\\n\\n2. **Design Principle: Logical Handoffs (AGENTS.md, line 18)**: \\\"Every stage produces a logical handoff... Neither surface exposes direct filesystem paths.\\\" Making struck state and entry IDs invisible to agents violates the principle of a complete handoff. The fix ensures the progressive-disclosure path carries all metadata needed for agents to correctly process retracted entries.\\n\\n3. **Agent Markdown Consumption Contract (agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md, line 12)**: \\\"An agent must never receive... silently truncated content.\\\" Struck entries without their metadata are exactly this kind of truncation. The fix brings the response shapes into compliance.\\n\\n4. **Historical Direction \u2014 Recent Merged Work**: Commits #3194 (\\\"resolve stored-id collisions on the structured read path\\\"), #3183 (\\\"honor the documented entry_id contract on the live write path\\\"), and #3165 (\\\"one rule for where an entry ends\\\") show a clear pattern over the past quarter of making entry metadata visible and fixing silent data loss in the progressive-disclosure path. This fix continues that established direction.\\n\\n**No contradictions found**: The proposed direction does not reverse any merged architectural decision or design principle. It reinforces the transparency and completeness of information agents receive.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287812Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given a section containing one struck entry and one live entry (the item's own reproduction shape), a `backlog_view` call using `map=True` or `navigate=` on that section returns both entries, and the struck entry is identifiable as struck through an explicit field in the response \u2014 not merely inferable from content or ordering.\\n2. Given the same reproduction, the live entry's corresponding field correctly indicates it is not struck \u2014 the distinction is bidirectional, not just a marker on one side.\\n3. Given the same reproduction, both entries are present in the response (count of returned entries equals count of input entries) \u2014 the struck entry is never silently dropped, matching the RT-ICA decision to always include, never omit.\\n4. Given a `navigate=` call at level-1 (section-aggregate granularity, the path currently handled by `ordinal_mapper.py::build_map()`'s content join), the response does not merge struck and live entry content into one undifferentiated string \u2014 the struck portion remains attributable to its originating entry and its struck state, whether that means it is separated, marked inline, or handled per the resolved design.\\n5. Given a `navigate=`+`head=` (EXTRACT mode) call that windows into a mixed section, the struck/live distinction established at criterion 1 remains observable in the extracted window, not just in the unwindowed full response.\\n6. Given a struck entry's unique ID as already produced by the `summary=False` (compact) path, the progressive-disclosure response for the same entry exposes a matching ID \u2014 cross-path consistency between the two read modes for the same underlying data.\\n7. Given a section with no struck entries at all (the common case), the response for that section is unchanged in entry count, content, and ordering from current behavior \u2014 this is a regression-safety criterion, not a struck-entry criterion.\\n8. Given the existing ~50 test methods in `test_ordinal_mapper.py` built on the `_make_sections`/`NormalizedEntry` fixture helper, they continue to construct their fixtures and pass without modification to their own assertions \u2014 only the fixture helper's construction call needs updating for the new fields, not the ~50 tests' individual expectations.\\n9. Given the `test_disclosure_handler.py` and `test_content_normalizer.py` suites, at least one test in each exercises a struck (`struck=True`) input end-to-end through the MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT response path and asserts on the new struck-identifying field, closing the zero-coverage gap impact-analyst identified in both files.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287794Z</sub>\\n\\nAfter the fix, a caller reading a section through `backlog_view` with `map=`/`navigate=` (at any depth: level-1 section aggregate, level-2 entry, or a sub-heading/code-fence terminal) observes struck entries as visibly and structurally distinguishable from live entries in the returned response \u2014 never presented as equal, undifferentiated peers, and never silently omitted from the response.\\n\\nThis holds per the item's own RT-ICA decision (condition 5, resolved via this repo's \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle and independently confirmed ALIGNED by the alignment-analyst against three separate design-principle sources): a struck entry is always included in the response, flagged as struck, never dropped and never presented as live. It also holds at the section-aggregate level currently handled by `ordinal_mapper.py::build_map()` \u2014 a caller resolving or navigating a section containing a mix of struck and live entries must be able to tell which portions of the returned content came from a struck entry, rather than receiving one undifferentiated joined string.\\n\\nThe `summary=False` (compact) path already exhibits this correct behavior today and is the reference behavior the progressive-disclosure path must match.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287879Z</sub>\\n\\n**M** (Medium) \u2014 paired with the Priority section's estimate. Root cause, fix locations, and full scope are already understood (RT-ICA: APPROVED, 0 missing conditions); the work is propagating one already-identified field through 4 non-test code files plus 1 docstring, updating 3 test files (one of which backs ~50 existing tests through a shared fixture helper), and refreshing 2 documentation surfaces with stale closed field-list enumerations \u2014 breadth, not investigation.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T12:03:54.287688Z</sub>\\n\\nThe item's Description and Reproduction sections already contain a concrete, deterministic reproduction. It calls `wrap_entry_with_timestamp`, `strike_entry`, and `parse_entries` directly as pure functions \u2014 no network call, no live backend read, no MCP round-trip, no filesystem I/O beyond what those functions already do internally. Running it twice on the same inputs produces the same output every time (confirmed by fact-checker's independent source trace of the same call chain).\\n\\nThis is sufficient reproducibility for a fix-validation cycle: the reproduction can be turned directly into a unit test assertion (input: one struck entry + one live entry with the same logical ID (`AC-1`) and contradictory content; expected: two independently addressable entries, each carrying its own struck state) without needing a running server,